# Crypto Watcher — Product Roadmap

> Source of truth for what we build and in which order. This document is referenced
> (cited) by [CONTRACTS](./CONTRACTS.mdc), [BACKEND](./BACKEND.mdc) and
> [FRONTEND](./FRONTEND.mdc). When a feature moves between phases, update this file
> first, then update the dependent docs.

## Product Summary

Crypto Watcher is a risk-analysis tool for crypto assets, projects and protocols.
It helps users decide whether a project is worth investing in based on risk and
potential reward. Core mechanics (see [README](../README.md)):

- Community risk/reward analysis through a **reputation-weighted, on-chain voting system**.
- Only trustworthy (reputation-gated) users may vote; voters are rewarded with tokens.
- A **governance token** is distributed to users and powers protocol governance.
- Each project has its own voting smart contract deployed on-chain.
- While voting is closed, anyone may comment (off-chain, public).
- Project lifecycle: `Deploy contract → In analysis by team → Ready for voting →
  Voting starts → Voting ends → Analyzed → Score calculated → Shown to users`.

> **Governance is a launch requirement.** Per the instructions, a minimum viable
> governance layer ships in the MVP and is hardened in every subsequent phase.
> Governance never appears as a "later" bolt-on.

## Phase Legend

Every feature in the dependent docs is tagged with one of the following so we can
prioritize:

- `MVP` — minimum lovable product, first public launch.
- `Phase 1` — trust & rewards.
- `Phase 2` — scale & decentralization.
- `Phase 3` — ecosystem & integrations.
- `Final` — maturity, full decentralization, sustainability.

---

## MVP — First Launch

Goal: a usable, end-to-end product where the team can list projects, the community
can comment, gated voters can vote on-chain, and scores are displayed. Ships with a
**minimal but real governance layer**.

### Core
- Project catalog: list, detail, lifecycle state (`In analysis → Ready → Voting →
  Closed → Analyzed`).
- Project score: computed from on-chain votes and shown to users.
- Public off-chain commenting on projects (read by everyone, post by authenticated users).
- Wallet connection (RainbowKit/Wagmi) and SIWE-style authentication.

### Voting
- Per-project voting smart contract (deploy + cast vote + close + tally).
- Backend-controlled project-submission factory: users request and pay for a
  review off-chain; only the platform backend may emit (deploy) a project's
  voting contract, recording the paying user as the on-chain submitter. The
  backend address is rotatable by governance, which can also pause submissions.
- Reputation-gated voting: only allow-listed / reputation-eligible wallets may vote.
- Reputation weight applied to votes (basic, single-factor model).

### Governance (launch requirement)
- Governance token contract (ERC-20 votes / `ERC20Votes`) deployed.
- Initial token distribution to a seed set of trustworthy users.
- Basic on-chain governance: proposal + vote + execution via a minimal Governor +
  Timelock, scoped to protocol parameters (e.g. voting duration, reputation threshold).
- Admin/team multisig as the timelock owner for safety during launch.

### Platform
- Auth, accounts, basic profile.
- Transactional emails (verification, notifications) via React Email + Nodemailer/MailHog.
- API documentation via Zod-generated OpenAPI + Swagger UI.
- CI/CD, security scanning (GitHub Actions), observability (Sentry, Better Stack).

---

## Phase 1 — Trust & Rewards

Goal: make reputation and incentives real and tamper-resistant.

- Reputation system v2: multi-factor scoring (voting accuracy, tenure, staking,
  participation), decay over time.
- Voter rewards: distribute governance tokens for honest/accurate votes.
- Slashing / penalties for malicious or low-quality voting.
- Reputation-based vote weight tiers (more trustworthy → more voting power).
- Governance hardening: token-weighted proposals open to the community,
  quorum + voting-delay parameters, on-chain parameter changes via Governor.
- Project submission flow for the team with review queue.
- Notification center (in-app + email) for lifecycle and governance events.

---

## Phase 2 — Scale & Decentralization

Goal: scale the platform and reduce reliance on the team.

- Gated review / curation of community project submissions (the open submission
  factory ships in MVP; this adds moderation, queues and economic gating).
- Multi-chain support for project voting contracts.
- Subgraph / indexer for on-chain data (replace direct RPC reads where needed).
- Delegated governance (vote delegation, `ERC20Votes` delegation UX).
- Reputation portability and richer analytics dashboards.
- Caching, rate limiting, and read-scaling on the backend.
- Role-based access control and team/admin console.

---

## Phase 3 — Ecosystem & Integrations

Goal: become a platform others build on.

- Public API & API keys for third parties.
- Webhooks for project/score/governance events.
- Embeddable score widgets / badges.
- Integrations: data providers (price/TVL/audit feeds) to enrich risk scoring.
- Mobile-friendly PWA / dedicated mobile experience.
- Advanced risk models (automated signals combined with community votes).

---

## Final — Maturity & Full Decentralization

Goal: a sustainable, community-owned protocol.

- Full DAO governance: treasury control, protocol upgrades, and parameter changes
  governed entirely on-chain by token holders.
- Decentralized reputation/identity (verifiable credentials).
- Self-serve project onboarding with economic anti-spam guarantees.
- Sustainable tokenomics and treasury management.
- Formal verification / audits of all core contracts.
- High-availability, multi-region infrastructure with SLAs.

---

## Cross-Cutting Concerns (all phases)

- **Governance**: present from MVP, deepened each phase (see above).
- **Security**: audits scale with phase; CI security scanning from MVP.
- **Observability**: Sentry + Better Stack from MVP.
- **Testing**: unit/integration/e2e from MVP (Vitest, Supertest, Testing Library,
  Playwright, Foundry).
- **Documentation**: keep `docs/` in sync with every implementation change
  (enforced by the docs-sync rule in `.cursor/rules/`).
